  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nA 1031-1033 mb high pressure over MO/IL and ridging from the mid\nMS river valley northward into western WI, was providing\nabundant sunshine and light to calm winds over central/se IL.\nTemps were mostly in the lower 50s with a few upper 40s over the\nIL river valley. Dry air mass prevailed with dewpoints in the\nupper 20s/lower 30s. Visible Satellite imagery shows\nstratocumulus clouds over central and eastern Indiana while\nnearly full sunshine over IL with few thin cirrus clouds over MS\nriver valley, sw of Jacksonville. An omega block pattern\ncontinued over the country with strong upper level lows in\nNewfoundland Canada and off the British Columbia/Alaskan coast.\nA weak 568 dm 500 mb low was over the Red River valley in sw OK\nwhile a skinny upper level ridge was over the Eastern Plains \njust west of IL. \n\nHigh pressure over IL tonight to bring fair skies, light to calm\nwinds and frosty/subfreezing low temperatures in the upper 20s \nto around 30F. The latest CAM runs like HRRR, RAP13 and to a\nlesser extent GFSLAMP showing patchy fog developing later\ntonight over ne and eastern parts of CWA (mainly east of I-57\nand ne of I-74) and near the IL river (more widespread ne of \nCWA), including pockets of dense fog. The shallow patchy fog \nshould lift quickly between 8-9 am Monday morning. \n\nHigh pressure ridge settles slowly across the Ohio river valley\nduring Monday with light southerly flow develop over central IL\nand light and variable winds yet in southeast IL. Ample sunshine\nMonday, though some high clouds increasing over IL river valley\nMonday afternoon, with highs in the mid 50s. Fair weather Monday\nnight with lows in the lower 30s. Ample sunshine on Tuesday with\nupper level ridge nosing over IL and surface high pressure\nshifting into the central Appalachians and mid Atlantic STates.\nCirculation around back side of surface high giving southerly \nwinds 5-15 mph warming temperatures into the upper 50s. Skies\nbecome partly to mostly sunny Wed with breezy south winds\ngusting 18-25 mph. A bit milder highs in the lower 60s, near \n60F from I-74 north. \n\nThe 12Z model suite has trended further north and nw with\npassage of a short wave trof ejecting ne into the upper Midwest\nWed night and Thu and appears to keep qpf nw of CWA overnight\nWed night and Thu. Highs in the low to mid 60s Thu with partly\nto mostly sunny skies and breezy sw winds gusting around 20 mph.\nMild Friday with highs in the mid to upper 60s with partly to\nmostly sunny skies (more sun expected in southeast IL), with\nlighter south winds 5-15 mph.\n\nUpper level ridge shifts over the Southeast States during 2nd\nhalf of the week. A stronger storm system to eject ne into the\nupper MS river valley by Saturday with a cold front moving se\nthrough IL late Sat afternoon and Sat night. Models have trended\nlighter with qpf amounts from this storm system over IL as it\nweakens as it moves into IN and southeast IL. NBM has 30-50%\nchance of showers on Saturday over central IL, especially in the\nafternoon, while still dry in southeast IL. 30-40% chance of\nshowers Sat night especially Sat evening, then trending drier\nand cooler on Sunday behind the cold front. Highs 65-70F Sat\n(mildest in southeast IL) cooling into low to mid 50s in central\nIL Sunday and 55-60F southeast IL.  \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for Nov \n23-29th with 35-45% chance of above normal temperatures over \ncentral/se IL and precipitation trending near normal.",
